# Trivy CVE exceptions for orkyo-foundation
#
# Format:
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN  # Impact | Reason | Added: YYYY-MM-DD | Review: YYYY-MM-DD
#
# Policy: time-bound only. Review date must be within 14 days of added date.
# Remove entries once the upstream fix is available.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-03: every Keycloak 26.6.x netty/jackson exception was cleared by the
# 26.7.2 pin (git history has the per-review narrative). Live entries follow.

# ── Trivy false positive ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Renewed on a 14-day cadence while it stays a false positive; a permanent-exception
# class (VEX) would end the churn — see the base-image-scan follow-ups.
CVE-2025-59250  # HIGH | mssql-jdbc: improper input validation — Trivy compares the installed 13.2.1 against a fix list ending 13.2.1.jre11 and calls it older; the shipped JAR is the fixed version, false positive (re-verified 2026-09-12 on quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:26.7.3) | Added: 2026-09-12 | Review: 2026-09-26

# ── Keycloak embedded Bouncy Castle — CVE-2026-8763 / CVE-2026-13506 ────────
# 2026-09-19: bcprov-jdk18on 1.84 is bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 (the pinned base) and by
# 26.7.4, the newest published tag — both scanned today with CI's flags, both report the
# same four findings, so a digest bump cannot clear this. The fix is bcprov 1.85 and no
# published Keycloak carries it. Production already runs this exact library: the pin has
# been 26.7.3 since 2026-09-12 and 0.23.0 shipped it, so holding releases does not reduce
# exposure. CVE-2026-8763 is an X.509 Name Constraints bypass in certificate path
# validation; this realm configures no x509 authenticator, no truststore and no mTLS, so
# Keycloak never validates a client certificate against a name-constrained CA. Keycloak
# also sits behind nginx and Cloudflare. Drop both entries when a Keycloak build with
# bcprov >= 1.85 is published; foundation #178 tracks it.
CVE-2026-8763  # CRITICAL | bcprov-jdk18on 1.84 bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 and 26.7.4 (fix 1.85) | no upstream Keycloak with the fix; production already runs this exact library; X.509 name-constraint path validation is unused here (no x509 authenticator, no truststore, no mTLS) | Added: 2026-09-19 | Review: 2026-09-26
CVE-2026-13506  # HIGH | bcprov-jdk18on 1.84, same library and same fix version as CVE-2026-8763 | Added: 2026-09-19 | Review: 2026-09-26

# ── Keycloak embedded netty — CVE-2026-75595 (CRITICAL) ─────────────────────
# 2026-09-12: netty-handler 4.1.136.Final is bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3, the newest
# upstream tag; the fix is 4.1.137/4.2.17 and no published Keycloak carries it, so a
# digest bump cannot clear this. Time-boxed as a deliberate risk decision, on one fact:
# production and staging ALREADY run this exact netty — the deployed 0.22.0 image
# (ghcr.io/kymr10n/keycloak:26.6-orkyo-0.22.0, digest dd1ae711…, scanned 2026-09-12)
# ships netty-handler-4.1.136.Final and reports this CVE. Holding the release train
# therefore does not reduce exposure; it only keeps the libexpat/openssl/util-linux base
# fixes and the 0.23.0 features out of production. Keycloak sits behind nginx and
# Cloudflare, not exposed directly. Drop this entry the moment a Keycloak build with
# netty ≥ 4.1.137 is published; foundation #176 tracks it.
CVE-2026-75595  # CRITICAL | netty-handler 4.1.136.Final bundled by Keycloak 26.7.3 (fix 4.1.137 / 4.2.17) | no upstream Keycloak with the fix yet; production already runs this exact netty (0.22.0 image, scanned 2026-09-12) so shipping is not a regression; KC behind nginx + Cloudflare | Added: 2026-09-12 | Review: 2026-09-26
